00:00:07.042 --> 00:00:08.750
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: As
you move to the cloud

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.570
and adopt the latest
technology, such as Kubernetes,

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.900
have you found it even harder
to find the root cause of issues

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.550
and understand just how well
your application is performing?

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.310
Stackdriver Application
Performance Management, a.k.a.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:24.295
APM, shines light
on your cloud native

00:00:24.295 --> 00:00:26.230
and multi-cloud
environments in order

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.540
to help you solve
these problems better.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.240
I'm Bryan Zimmerman, Product
Manager for Trace and Debugger.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.448
MORGAN MCLEAN: And I'm Morgan
McLean, Product Manager

00:00:33.448 --> 00:00:34.758
for Stackdriver Profiler.

00:00:34.758 --> 00:00:37.300
Stackdriver is Google's suite
of management and observability

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:37.960
products.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.270
APM is focused on
that deeper levels

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.910
of debug-style
information to help

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.250
reduce mean time to resolution,
find performance optimizations,

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.950
and build the best possible
experience for your users

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.710
APM features three
products: Stackdriver Trace,

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:53.022
Stackdriver Profiler,
and Stackdriver Debugger.

00:00:53.022 --> 00:00:55.480
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: Trace is our
distributing tracing product.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.450
What is distributed
tracing, you ask?

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.550
Well tracing allows you to
visualize how a request flows

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.150
through your environment.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:04.420
In microservices, applications,
or highly distributed

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.660
environments, understanding
context and flow

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.882
is essential to
resolving problems.

00:01:09.882 --> 00:01:11.840
MORGAN MCLEAN: How do we
use this in real life?

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:12.880
For example, let's
assume that we

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.860
receive an alert that
shows a jump in latency

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.420
for our application.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.310
Trace gives us the
best detailed data

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:19.865
to help us troubleshoot
this error.

00:01:19.865 --> 00:01:21.490
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: With
Stackdriver Trace

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:23.860
you can easily see
the top requests,

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.190
RPC calls and
associated latency,

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.040
find an example of the issue
you having with simple search

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.060
and filter, and see a
simple view of the request

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.650
as it processes through
your complex environment.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.715
This includes detailed custom
information and annotations.

00:01:40.715 --> 00:01:42.590
MORGAN MCLEAN: For more
detailed information,

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.037
analysis reports are
generated automatically.

00:01:45.037 --> 00:01:47.120
You can view an existing
report or create your own

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.530
to see how latency
has changed over time.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.100
You can group
these by percentile

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.300
and you can also drill
down into example traces

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.087
to find out exactly what
happened to cause this issue.

00:01:56.087 --> 00:01:58.670
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: As you can see,
Trace guides you from problem

00:01:58.670 --> 00:01:59.900
to service.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:02.640
Now we will show you how you
can get from service to method

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:03.723
with Stackdriver Profiler.

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:05.973
MORGAN MCLEAN: Profiler can
inspect the CPU and memory

00:02:05.973 --> 00:02:08.060
performance of all the
functions in your code,

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.460
and it shows you the calling
relationships between them.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.160
Profiler can visualize your
code on a flame graph, which

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.680
shows calling relationships
along the vertical axis

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.953
and resource consumption
on the horizontal axis.

00:02:17.953 --> 00:02:20.120
You can use a number of
different filters, including

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:21.860
weight filtering,
which only shows

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.260
data captured during
periods of high consumption,

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.540
and a focused filter, which
shows all of the paths

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.683
in and out of a
selected function.

00:02:28.683 --> 00:02:31.100
If you need to visualize the
performance of functions that

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.642
are called throughout
your code base,

00:02:32.642 --> 00:02:34.190
you could use the
focus table, which

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.810
shows the aggregated cause of
all the functions in the graph.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.050
Finally, once you've
identified the area of code

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:40.550
that you're interested
in, it's time

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:43.038
to inspect the behavior of
this code to find the problem.

00:02:43.038 --> 00:02:45.080
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: This is
where Debugger comes in.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.970
Stackdriver Debugger
offers the ability

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:50.270
to take log points and
snapshots of your running code,

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:54.020
in production, without impact,
redeploying, or restarting

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:55.760
your services.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.740
Traditionally,
debugging involves

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:01.670
analyzing logs and metrics,
taking a guess at the problem,

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:05.060
deploying some new code or debug
module, looking at the logs

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.090
again to confirm,
waiting for the issue,

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.210
analyzing logs again, perhaps
taking another crack at it.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:13.100
Stackdriver Debugger
allows you to take

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.800
breakpoints and
snapshots in production

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.860
without system impact,
redeploying, or restarting

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:19.783
your application.

00:03:19.783 --> 00:03:21.950
This allows you to tighten
your troubleshooting flow

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.050
significantly, as you're
not having to wait

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.370
for lengthy deployments.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.320
In addition to the
efficiency gains,

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.650
many customers report being
able to bring debugging further

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.610
up the troubleshooting
stack to operators or SREs,

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.158
further reducing MTTR and
affecting customer impact.

00:03:39.158 --> 00:03:41.700
MORGAN MCLEAN: All of these APM
tools work with code and apps

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.350
that are running on
any cloud, or even

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:44.520
On-Premise infrastructure.

